================================================================================
            
                                ENCODER NOTES

================================================================================

*********************************** BUGS ***************************************


******************************** IMPROVEMENTS **********************************

1. "dribbling" of registers can be eliminated by 1. separating the push/pop mask
into different push and pop masks, then 2. introducing a separate pass that
finds dribbled registers in intermediates and eliminates all of the middle push
and pop orders. Do this along with statistics so we can tell how effective it 
is.

2. Implement the "long distance" cache for registers. In this method, each
intermediate gets a "copy order" that states one register is copied to another
(or a pair). The long distance cache is then a structure that holds loaded
values or addresses as a pointer to their intermediates. It is cleared when
normal context is cleared, and also when all registers are allocated (meaning
that there would be no extra registers to carry the value forward). When an
item is found in the long distance cache, the original intermediate gets a copy
order to an extra register, the item is placed in the normal cache, and 
allocation continues.

3. Reintegrate N-length sets.

[there are actually unresolved issues with this. When building a set, the system
loads a nil set, then executes single and range sets into the empty set. There
is no way to know how big the set is before the operation is done, nor what its
offset is. One possibility is to stack up the set orders, keeping min and max
numbers on them, then create the set and execute the set orders into it.]

4. Need option to critically pack record fields.

[difficult]

5. The intermediate tree is treated in most areas as a single way linked list.
"up" entries already exist for the intermediate list, and "back" links would be
easy to add. This would clean up the code considerably for delete/replace cases
in the list.

6. Finish graph operators document.
 
7. "for" loop unrolling needs to be moved up to the intermediate level. This
involves making an intermediate copy that can both take full graphs, and
duplicate jump/label pairs.

8. Add a system to page out floating points to temps so that overflows won't
occur. As it is, the system is designed with a fixed limit of 8.

9. Prevent thrashing when multiple operands of an intermediate use the same
register, ie., stop multiple push/pops under the same intermediate. 

[This is addressed by 1]

10. Detect constant for loops used to set arrays, and implement more efficient
vectorized sets for this.

11. Implement write of enumerated types. This can be done by storing all the ids
of an enumerated type into the runtime, then changing the write of an enumerated
to a cased string write.

12. Is there a way to suppress the system block from being output ?

13. Implement sorts for global variables, locals, and possibly record fields.
This will remove the inefficiencies of alignments.

14. Restrict mode needs effort to work with signing. By moving restriction
points down to loads, it creates the situation were a load must also perform
any bounds checking. This is an issue with the many routines like gendoi that
can directly code such loads into an instruction.

15. Upgrade the context system to handle double register allocations.

16. It might be more effective to fill the skippy trip countermeasures with
false, but apparently valid, code. This could be a mode that is invoked
at random.

17. One reveng cm that would be effective is to form calls by pushing the
return address on the stack, then jumping to the routine. What this allows is
to put skippy trips or other trash in after the jump, and the return resumes
after that.

18. There is nothing technically wrong with providing overrides of any nested
virtual with any other override. This would give the ability for a overrider
to access local variables even during the override. In any case, the virtual
vectors would have to be locals, and currently the compiler is not set up for
that.

************************************ 2010 OVERHAUL *****************************

The 2010 overhaul candidates follow.

1. Breakdown products. The workings of this were detailed in "brkdown.txt". 
Basically it means to convert standard intermediates to simpler, lower level
(but still machine indepenent) intermediates to simplify register allocation
and code generation.

2. Moving the "for" loop unrolling up a level as was previously described. This
dovetails into other initiatives detailed here.

3. Structure rip out. In this idea, all of the structures would be devolved into
jumps in the final code. This would, in fact, flatten all of the code outside
expression trees. All of the structure handling could then be removed from the
machine code section. This flatten operation would be called by the machine
module to accomodate generators that wish to keep the structures.

4. Expression tree flattening. A pass is made to flatten the expression trees.
This would allow the entire intermediate to be a linear list. It is dependent on
not having any use in the generator routines for subtrees, which the breakdown
products idea would enable. This would greatly simplify the machine module.

Lcc also has the idea that the intermediate is linked into a double way linear
list, outside of the tree structure. This leaves the tree intact, and allows
dual way traversing of the intermediate, for, say, "distance" of use 
calculations.

5. Register allocation. The "logical registers" plan used in the lcc compiler 
would be used. Then, we would use a "briging" pass to enable the temps to be
replaced by push/pop sequences.

We can also investigate register coloring.

6. N-Sets. It should be fairly simple to integrate N-sets that are zero based.
This is becoming a problem with Unicode and also the increasing sizes of the
enumerations IP uses for itself. See comments above.

7. Record and array packing. This is not easy, but worth it, since it allows
arbitrary structure representation (like being able to break out IEEE floats in
their native format), and does interesting things like bring arrays of bits to
the same level as sets.